#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121136 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ is parametrized by arc length $s,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) =  \\left\\| \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}s}  \\right\\|.\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ we have the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nIt can be shown that the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t)$ can also be found using the following cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson. For now, let's get some practice at applying it." :tutorial/id #uuid "699ec19c-b03f-5cc8-8096-613245aa4961" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121137 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane curve $C$ that is parametrized by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks as though the cross product curvature formula cannot be applied since cross products apply to three-dimensional vectors, whereas the given vector $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has only two dimensions.\n\nHowever, this curve can also be viewed as a curve in three-dimensional space that lies on the plane $z=0.$ In this case, $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ can be considered as a vector-valued function with $z$-component equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle x(t), \\: y(t), \\: 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAs a result, the curvature $\\kappa$ of our plane curve $C$ can be calculated using our cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t)=\\dfrac{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\times {\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime\\prime}(t)}\\|}{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\|^3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2ae1ba4c-311d-5298-8526-146a426bda14" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7884 :tutorial/title "Applying the Cross Product Formula to Plane Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121138 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nHere, $\\mathbf r'(t)$ is a vector function and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$ is a scalar function.\nSo, we differentiate $\\mathbf T(t)$ by using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(T) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({r}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{″}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the cross product of a vector with itself equals $\\mathbf{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\times {T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\times (\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{″}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,({r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of both sides and use the fact that $\\mathbf T$ is a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖T(t)\\times {T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖T(t)‖\\cdot ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ \\\\ 1\\cdot ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|} = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f59936dc-ef8e-53d7-be8e-244d577dd658" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7885 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Cross Product Formula"}]}
